key city enum<string>['USSFO', 'USNYC', 'USBOS', 'FRPAR', 'USBTV', 'CAVAN', 'DEBER', 'NLAMS', 'GBLON', 'AUMEL', 'ARBUE', 'USLAX', 'USWAS', 'USTEM', 'GRATH', 'GRMLO', 'GRSAN', 'USDEN', 'CACAL', 'CAEDM', 'CAWPG', 'CATOR', 'CAMTL', 'CAQUE', 'CALON', 'CAHFX', 'CAKGN', 'CALET', 'CAVIC', 'CAKEL', 'CANWE', 'CAMIS', 'CAOTT', 'CABUR', 'CAAJX', 'CAMON', 'JPTYO', 'COBOG', 'TWTPE', 'DKCPH', 'FIHEL', 'USSTO']; # UN/LOCODE-style code for the source city

# The dataset each tree row came from is modelled as a *per-city* enum key
# (`ussfo_source`, `usbos_source`, …) declared in each city's tree model, not as
# one global key here. Trilogy proves a city's Parquet is complete by checking
# that its raw sources cover every value of the partitioning enum, so a global
# 30-value enum is never covered by any single city's two sources and the whole
# model fails to resolve — verified, as does a plain `key data_source string`.
# Each city aliases its key to the physical column `data_source`, so every tree
# Parquet still exposes one uniformly named column. The full value list lives in
# DATA_SOURCES in data/raw/shared/ingest.py; data/raw/tests/test_data_sources.py
# asserts the two stay in sync.

# The data_source / submission_photo_url additions did not need a bump: the
# deployed app selects a strict subset of the new columns, so an old app reading
# a rebuilt parquet is fine, and rolling the app back stays fine. Boston drops
# `usbos_source` in favour of `data_source`, but nothing outside the preql models
# ever read that column. A bump would also have forced the LLM-backed enrichment
# parquet and every landmark parquet to re-materialize for no reason, since they
# all share this one param.
#
# The ordering that does matter: refresh the parquets before deploying the app.
# The new worker selects data_source / submission_photo_url by name, so it fails
# to load against a not-yet-rebuilt parquet.
param data_version string default '2';

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Publish guards
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A published datasource carries `assert` clauses that are checked on every
# build before it replaces its parquet: `prior(...)` is the same aggregate over
# the parquet about to be replaced.  A failing build leaves the published
# parquet untouched and fails the run, which is what a portal that suddenly
# serves 100 rows instead of 1.47M (Bogotá, 2026-10-13) needs.  The tree and
# landmark templates (tree_dedup.preql, landmark_common.preql) carry `kept`, and
# each city target adds `source_kept` per municipal and OSM label.
#
# Ratios, not floors: no number here has to be revised as a city grows.  A
# quarter is well clear of a normal rebuild (inventories gain trees; OSM and
# reviewed imagery are absorbed a few at a time) and well short of a collapse.
#
# Edges, from pytrilogy: the first build of an asset has no prior and passes
# with a warning; a schema change does too.  Counts are compared filtered, never
# `by data_source`, because a group missing from the new build always fails,
# and a city's community or satellite rows can legitimately all be absorbed.
#
# A drop known to be real (a portal migration that sheds rows) is built once,
# locally, with `trilogy refresh <file> --skip-validation <datasource>`; that
# build becomes the next one's prior.  Cloud jobs cannot pass the flag.
#
# `prior` needs pytrilogy 0.3.389 (0.3.391 to check it on a partial city
# target), and enrichment/_tree_shared.py parses this
# file at import, so every uv script that imports it pins
# `pytrilogy>=0.3.391`: an unpinned one reuses whatever uv has cached.
def kept(key, pct) -> count(key) >= pct * prior(count(key));
# The cross-city rollups compare city by city, so one city's parquet missing
# from the scan fails the build rather than quietly emptying that city.
def kept_by(key, dim, pct) -> count(key) by dim >= pct * prior(count(key) by dim);
def source_kept(key, source, label, pct) -> count(key ? source = label) >= pct * prior(count(key ? source = label));
